{hline}

{title:Title}

{p2colset 5 18 20 2}{...}
{p2col:{cmd:bootdhreg} {hline 2}}Double-hurdle regression: Bootstrap estimator{p_end}


{title:Syntax}

{p 8 17 2}
{cmdab:bootdhreg}
{it:depvar}
{it:indepvars}
{ifin}
[{cmd:,} {it:options}]

{synoptset 20}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{synopt:{opt up}}choose upper instead of lower hurdle{p_end}
{synopt:{opt ptobit}}estimate only intercept of hurdle{p_end}
{synopt:{opt hd(varlist)}}specify that hurdle be explained with different {it:indepvars}{p_end}
{synopt:{opt millr}}estimate second version with inverse Mills ratio controlling for correlation of error terms{p_end}
{synopt:{opt margins(string)}}call for margins{p_end}
{synopt:{opt seed(integer)}}set seed for randomization{p_end}
{synopt:{opt reps(integer)}}define number of bootstrap repetitions{p_end}
{synopt:{opt strata(varlist)}}define bootstrap strata{p_end}
{synopt:{opt cluster(varlist)}}define bootstrap clusters{p_end}
{synopt:{opt capt}}prevent bootstrap from ending if single instance does not converge{p_end}
{synopt:{opt maxiter(integer)}}limit number of iterations per bootstrap{p_end}
{synoptline}
{p2colreset}{...}


{title:Description}

{pstd}
{cmd:bootdhreg} estimates double-hurdle regression using
bootstrapping.


{title:Options}

{phang}
{opt up} specifies that the upper, not the lower, limit of the support of the
dependent variable be treated as the hurdle.

{phang}
{opt ptobit} estimates the equation for the outer hurdle with just the
intercept.

{phang}
{opt hd(varlist)} allows a set of explanatory variables for the outer hurdle
that differs from the explanatory variables for the inner hurdle and those
realizations of the dependent variable that surmount the hurdle.

{phang}
{opt millr} estimates a second version of the model with the inverse Mills
ratio controlling for potential correlation of the error terms.

{phang}
{opt margins(string)} calls for bootstrap estimates of marginal effects.

{phang}
{opt seed(integer)} fixes a seed for the randomization (as a default, one seed
is implemented so that results can be replicated).

{phang}
{opt reps(integer)} defines the number of bootstrap repetitions.  The default
is {cmd:reps(50)}.

{phang}
{opt strata(varlist)} orders the bootstrap to be stratified.

{phang}
{opt cluster(varlist)} defines higher-order aggregates from which samples are
drawn.

{phang}
{opt capt} ignores repetitions that do not converge.  It may be useful if, for
instance, the program does not converge for some samples because of
clustering.  The bootstrap results are then taken from the remaining draws.

{phang}
{opt maxiter(integer)} limits the number of iterations.  {cmd:maxiter()} is
also useful if the maximum likelihood routine has a hard time converging.
{cmd:maxiter()} should be combined with {cmd:capt}.  The default is
{cmd:maxiter(50)}.


{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
This is the bootstrap version of {cmd:dhreg}.  The model assumes a double
hurdle.  Some observations are systematically different and therefore
categorically excluded from passing the hurdle.  Others might in principle
pass.  Explanatory variables are such that they nonetheless stay before the
hurdle.  Probit and tobit models from which starting values for maximum
likelihood estimation are derived are also displayed.

{pstd}
If {cmd:millr} is selected, output consists of two sets.  Without this option,
the estimator assumes that the error terms of the hurdle equation and the
error term of the estimation conditional on the first hurdle being passed are
uncorrelated.

{pstd}
With the option, there is a second set of output.  This second set assumes
that error terms are correlated.  In the spirit of Heckman's two-step
estimator, the estimator treats the correlation between error terms as a
missing variable.  From the first maximum likelihood estimation (not from the
probit model used for getting starting values), the estimator derives the
inverse Mills ratio, that is, the probability density function (at predicted
values), divided by the cumulative density function (again at predicted
values).  This additional parameter is used as an additional regressor in the
second set of maximum likelihood estimates.

{pstd}
It is suggested that users check the p-value of the coefficient.  If it is
clearly insignificant, this indicates that error terms are indeed not
correlated.  In that case, the first set of regressors is more adequate.

{pstd}
The estimator is prepared to calculate marginal effects in postestimation
using {cmd:margins()}.  Stata treats the coefficients conditional on the first
hurdle being passed as {cmd:equation(above)} or as {cmd:equation(below)} if
{cmd:up} is chosen.  Coefficients of the first hurdle are treated as
{cmd:equation(hurdle)}.  If you wish to calculate marginal effects assuming
that error terms are uncorrelated (if appropriate as a first step), run the
estimator without {cmd:millr}.

{pstd}
The dependent variable should not have missing values.  When the auxiliary
variable for the first hurdle is generated, the missing values would be
treated as positive expressions.  Users should first create a dataset where
they drop these observations.

{pstd}
The bootstrap version of the estimator is helpful if the user suspects
heteroskedasticity.  It may also help with panel and nested data.  If the
panel has only one specific error term, the random-effects version of the
estimator {cmd:xtdhreg} will usually be preferable.  Yet it of course assumes
that the individual-specific error term is uncorrelated with the fixed portion
of the model.  Given there is no generally acknowledged fixed-effects version
of tobit models on which the double-hurdle model relies, this assumption
cannot be tested using the {cmd:hausman} test.  Therefore, using
{cmd:bootdhreg} may also serve as a safeguard.  If users have panel data, they
should use {cmd:cluster()}.  Defining the cluster at the highest level of
dependence, for example, groups if there are data from individuals interacting
repeatedly within fixed groups.

{pstd}
The bootstrap estimator also provides bootstrap estimates of the standard
errors of marginal effects.  Users must then fully specify the margins they
ask for in {cmd:margins()} together with the main command, not in
postestimation.

{pstd}
The bootstrap procedure can be customized with {cmd:strata()}, {cmd:reps()},
and {cmd:seed()}.  {cmd:capt} should be used only if, on a first attempt, the
bootstrap procedure could not be finished because {cmd:dhreg} did not converge
on some random samples.  {cmd:maxiter} goes even one step further and forces
approximation to finish after a defined number of iterations.  It should be
used responsibly.

{pstd}
The estimator makes {cmd:_res}{it:*} variables available.  These are
coefficients from individual bootstrap instances.  These estimates can be
useful for postestimation.


{title:Examples}

{phang}{cmd:. bootdhreg contribution riskaversion received_punishment}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. bootdhreg contribution riskaversion received_punishment, hd(major) up reps(500)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. bootdhreg contribution riskaversion received_punishment, ptobit cluster(uid)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. bootdhreg contribution riskaversion i.treatment##c.received_punishment, margins(dydx(*) predict(equation(hurdle)))}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. bootdhreg contribution riskaversion i.treatment##c.period, margins(dydx(treat) over(period) predict(equation(above)))}{p_end}


{marker saved_results}{...}
{title:Stored results}

{pstd}
{cmd:bootdhreg} stores the following in {cmd:e()}:

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(N)}}number of observations{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k)}}number of parameters{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k_eq)}}number of equations in {cmd:e(b)}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k_eq_model)}}number of equations in overall model test{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k_dv)}}number of dependent variables{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(df_m)}}model degrees of freedom{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ll)}}log likelihood{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(chi2)}}chi-squared{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(p)}}significance{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(rank)}}rank of {cmd:e(V)}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ic)}}number of iterations{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(rc)}}return code{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(converged)}}{cmd:1} if converged, {cmd:0} otherwise{p_end}

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Macros}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(cmd)}}{cmd:bootdhreg}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(depvar)}}name of dependent variable{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(chi2type)}}{cmd:Wald} or {cmd:LR}; type of model chi-squared test{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(vce)}}{it:vcetype} specified in {cmd:vce()}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(opt)}}type of optimization{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(which)}}{cmd:max} or {cmd:min}; whether optimizer is to perform maximization or minimization{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ml_method)}}type of {cmd:ml} method{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(user)}}name of likelihood-evaluator program{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(technique)}}maximization technique{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(properties)}}{cmd:b V}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(predict)}}program used to implement {cmd:predict}{p_end}

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Matrices}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(b)}}coefficient vector{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(Cns)}}constraints matrix{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ilog)}}iteration log{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(gradient)}}gradient vector{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(V)}}variance-covariance matrix of the estimators{p_end}


